## Before every commit

1. Run `just ci` and fix what fails. Never weaken a check to get it passing: no lowered lint
   level, no new exception without a reason that holds up, no skipped or deleted test.
2. Spawn a fresh-context review subagent. Give it the diff (`git diff --cached`, or
   `git diff main...HEAD` for a branch), the task it implements, [docs/style.md](docs/style.md),
   the [Code](#code) section of this file and the
   [Google Rust Style Guide](https://google.github.io/styleguide/rustguide.html), and none of your
   own reasoning. Ask it to check the diff against the review checklist in `docs/style.md` and the
   Code section, and report findings with file and line.
3. Fix every finding that holds up, rerun `just ci`, and repeat the review if the fixes were
   more than mechanical.
4. Commit with a [conventional commit](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) message, one
   coherent change per commit.
5. Push to a branch and open a pull request for the user. Never push to `main`, merge a pull
   request, or change the repository's settings; the user does those. Don't run the owner's
   recipes, `just setup-github` and `just check-github`, either.

## Silencing a lint

Put an `expect` with a reason on the smallest item the lint applies to:

- a clippy or rustc lint: `#[expect(clippy::lint_name, reason = "...")]`;
- a custom lint: `#[cfg_attr(dylint_lib = "trellis_lints", expect(lint_name, reason = "..."))]`.
  The `cfg_attr` keeps ordinary builds, which don't know the lint, from warning about it.

That is the only way. `allow`, `warn`, inner `#![...]` attributes, lint groups, `#[clippy::...]`
attributes, a short reason or one that only spells out the lint's name, and lint levels that an
untrusted macro writes all fail `just dylint`, and some lints take no exception at all.
[docs/style.md](docs/style.md#silencing-a-lint) has the full rules.

If a rule seems wrong for this project, say so to the user rather than changing it. A rule
changes only when the user asks, in a pull request of its own.

## Where a rule belongs

The repository is the next agent's memory: whatever pattern is in it gets copied, a workaround
included. So a rule lives as far up this list as it can, strongest first:

1. **Structure.** Types, crate boundaries and APIs that make the wrong thing impossible to write.
2. **Checks.** A lint level, a cargo-deny rule, a structure rule in `[workspace.metadata.policy]`,
   or a test.
3. **This file.** Guidance for what no check can decide, such as product boundaries.
4. **Review.** The checklist in [docs/style.md](docs/style.md).

When a review finding comes up a second time, move it up. A new custom lint or structure check
belongs in [trellis](https://github.com/265866/trellis), so every project gets it; ask the user
to add it there. A pull request can't change rules and code together;
[CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md#send-a-change) has the order for a new rule that flags existing
code and for a new proc macro.

## Comments

Rust code has no `//` or `/* */` comments except `// SAFETY:` ones; the `code_comment` lint
enforces it, and [docs/style.md](docs/style.md#where-trellis-goes-further-than-the-guide) says why.
Put the why where it is checked or reviewed with the code: a doc comment on the item, the name of
a function or test, an assertion message, or the `reason` of an `#[expect(...)]`. Before you reuse
a workaround you find, even one an `expect` reason explains, check that the problem it works
around still exists. Configuration files (TOML, YAML, the justfile) keep their comments, since
they have nowhere else to say why.

## Code

- **Errors.** A failure that a caller can recover from returns a `Result` whose error says what
  failed and with what input. Never panic on user input, I/O or anything over the network; a
  panic is for a bug or a broken invariant.
- **Fail closed.** Input the code doesn't recognize is an error, not a default.
- **Logs.** No secret, token or personal data in a log line or an error message.
- **Tests.** A test of timeouts, expiry, ordering or retries uses a clock it controls or explicit
  timestamps, never a sleep. Fixtures hold made-up data, never real names, emails, addresses,
  keys or tokens.

## New crates

Each crate has one job. `just policy` checks every crate against the rules
[docs/style.md](docs/style.md#checked-by-tools) lists under `trellis-policy check`. When you add
one:

- **Place and manifest.** Put it under `crates/`. Its `[lints]` table is exactly
  `workspace = true`, and it inherits `edition`, `rust-version` and `publish` from the workspace.
- **Settings.** Give it an entry in `internal_dependencies` in `[workspace.metadata.policy]`,
  which lists the workspace crates it may depend on directly, and list it in `changelog_include`
  in `release-plz.toml`, so its commits reach the release notes. A build script needs the crate in
  `build_script_crates`, and a direct dependency on a proc-macro crate needs that crate in
  `proc_macro_crates`. These settings are rules, so a new crate or a new edge between crates is a
  rule change.
{%- if kind == 'library' and publish_crate %}
- **Publishing.** Every crate is published to crates.io. A new crate needs a first manual publish
  and a trusted publisher on crates.io before the next release. That is the owner's work, so
  tell the user when you add a crate;
  [docs/maintaining.md](docs/maintaining.md#add-a-crate-to-a-published-library) has the steps.
{%- endif %}
- **Agent instructions.** This file is the only one for coding agents. Any other `AGENTS.md`, or a
  `CLAUDE.md`, `GEMINI.md`, `.cursorrules`, `.cursor/`, `.claude/` or
  `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, fails `just policy`.

